References: <CAG6L79noZAQ_pYjbZgAkxaH=Hs8-+5QVc1MJ0whNQD4N-qoD8A@mail.gmail.com>
Message-ID: <CABfftS2hJEExw3oANYKJz=+vTzpwNFcPNqSLp2B0oho7p+GubQ@mail.gmail.com>

Hi David:

More on Zelig 4 scoping rules can be found here:
https://lists.gking.harvard.edu/pipermail/zelig/2013-May/001190.html.

In Zelig 5, the error was coming from a flag not set to silent in
“wrappers.R” and was not affecting further code execution. So

mf <- try(eval(mf, environment()))

is now

mf <- try(eval(mf, environment()), silent = TRUE)

Running your example, we get:

rm(list = ls())
test_function <- function() {
  data(cars)
  x <- cars
  zelig(dist ~ speed, data = x, model = "ls", cite = FALSE)}
test_function()

## Model:
## $by
## [1] 1
##
##
## Call:
## stats::lm(formula = formula, data = .)
##
## Coefficients:
## (Intercept)        speed
##     -17.579        3.932
##
## Next step: Use 'setx' method

(https://github.com/IQSS/Zelig/issues/49)

Best regards,
Christine


